---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About - Andrew Giffen"
  description="Product leader in regulated enterprise software. A decade getting software - and now AI - into production inside compliance-heavy enterprises."
>
  <section class="about-page">
    <header class="page-header">
      <p class="page-label">About</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">
        I'm Andrew Giffen. For ten years I've been getting software to work
        inside regulated enterprises. Lately, that software is AI.
      </h1>
    </header>

    <div class="about-content">
      <div class="about-narrative">
        <p>
          I started on the customer side of enterprise software, in partner enablement
          and business relations for compliance platforms, working with enterprise
          customers on integrations and implementations. That's where I learned that
          enterprise software rarely fails on features. It fails on fit: the workflow it
          has to slot into, the systems it has to talk to, the auditor who has to trust it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Since 2018 I've run product at a global compliance SaaS company, growing the
          product organization from one person to about two dozen PMs, POs, and program
          managers. When the company restructured, I stepped in as interim CTO for eight
          months - stabilized delivery, shipped a new authoring platform, and created the
          competitive-intelligence application that became the company's flagship product.
        </p>

        <p>
          More recently I've been building production AI inside that platform myself. The
          EHS Applicability Engine runs 500+ facility-level screening questions in batch and
          returns structured decisions auditors act on. PM Insights analyzes 74,000+ recorded
          calls and 2.5 million transcript sentences, and the product team prioritizes against
          it every week. Neither stopped at a pilot. Getting them into production was mostly
          about trust: traceable reasoning, a human in the loop, and answers people could
          defend to a regulator.
        </p>

        <p>
          After hours I build my own products with Claude Code - a peer-support app on the
          App Store, a song-level music graph, a timing trainer for bass players. They're at
          <a href="https://www.giffenlabs.ca">giffenlabs.ca</a>. They keep my hands on the
          tools, and they're where I find out what AI can actually do before I bring it to
          work.
        </p>

        <p>
          The work I want more of is the hard middle of enterprise AI: sitting with a
          customer, finding where AI fits in the real workflow, and getting it from pilot to
          production - building what's needed along the way. I'm based in Toronto:
          <a href="mailto:andrew@giffen.me">andrew@giffen.me</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-section">
        <h2 class="section-heading">How I work</h2>
        <div class="principles">
          <div class="principle">
            <h3>Production, not pilots</h3>
            <p>
              A pilot proves the model can do the job. Production proves the customer
              trusts it to. Most enterprise AI stalls in between, and the gap is rarely
              the model. It's the workflow, the integration, and the audit trail. That
              gap is where I like to work.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="principle">
            <h3>Build it to understand it</h3>
            <p>
              I don't spec what I haven't touched. Building a rough version myself is the
              fastest way to find out where the hard parts really are, and it makes me a
              better partner to the engineers who build the real one.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="principle">
            <h3>Lead with the real problem</h3>
            <p>
              I don't start with solutions. Before anything gets on a roadmap, I want
              to understand what's actually broken - for the customer, for the
              business, for the people doing the work. Most product failures I've
              seen weren't execution failures. They were diagnosis failures. We built
              the wrong thing well.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="principle">
            <h3>Write to align, not to document</h3>
            <p>
              My best thinking happens on paper before it happens in a room. I write
              strategy docs, decision memos, and one-pagers because the act of writing
              forces clarity. When a team can read a document and execute without me
              in the room, that's leverage.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="principle">
            <h3>Ship to learn - and to de-risk</h3>
            <p>
              Speed matters, but so does direction. I bias toward shipping small
              working things over perfect large ones. Not because I don't care about
              quality - because I've seen too many long-horizon plans collapse the
              moment they hit real users. A working prototype tells you more in a day
              than a roadmap tells you in a month.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="principle">
            <h3>Make complexity legible</h3>
            <p>
              Regulated industries are full of complexity that experts have stopped
              noticing. My job - in product, in strategy, in AI - is to find the
              underlying structure in that complexity and make it something a team can
              act on. Find the structure, surface it, act on it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-page {
    padding: var(--space-10) var(--space-7);
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .page-header {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-9);
  }

  .page-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--accent);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .page-label::before {
    content: '';
    width: 32px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--accent);
  }

  .page-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-3xl);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--ink);
    line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .about-narrative {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-9);
  }

  .about-narrative p {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
    max-width: 65ch;
  }

  .about-narrative a {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .about-narrative a:hover {
    color: var(--accent-light);
  }

  .about-section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-9);
    padding-top: var(--space-8);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--paper-edge);
  }

  .section-heading {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .principles {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-7);
  }

  .principle h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .principle p {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .about-page {
      padding: var(--space-9) var(--space-5);
    }

    .page-title {
      font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    }

    .principles {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
